Ladybird’s storage.session keeps its values in memory, under the
10,485,760-byte quota, and opens itself to content scripts only after
setAccessLevel(). Until then, it refuses their calls with Chromium’s
“Access to storage is not allowed from this context.” in the same
wording, since extensions compare it. Chromium’s SettingsFunction::
PreRunValidation() makes that same check, WebKit keeps the level per
extension (WebExtensionStorageAccessLevel, which its
WebExtensionContextAPIStorage.cpp checks), and Gecko’s ext-storage.js
keeps the session area in memory as well.
